<BaseLayout
  title="How We Select Products and Guide Topics"
  seoTitle="How We Select Products and Guide Topics"
  description="How AHC Product Research assesses suitability, evidence, safety, drawbacks and approval before a product can receive affiliate placement."
>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <div class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Our recommendation methodology</p>
      <h1>How We Select Products and Guide Topics</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A recommendation begins with a household decision, not a product we want to feature.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="content narrow">
    <h2>How we develop recommendations</h2>
    <p>
      Our recommendations are based on practical suitability rather than product popularity,
      marketing claims, sales rankings or commission rates.
    </p>
    <p>Instead of asking only which product is best, we ask:</p>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li>Will it suit the reader's home and intended use?</li>
      <li>Will it fit the available space and delivery route?</li>
      <li>What is required to install and use it properly?</li>
      <li>What trade-offs should the buyer understand?</li>
      <li>What may matter during ownership and after-sales support?</li>
      <li>What commonly overlooked problems can be avoided?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Our goal is to give readers a useful framework for their own circumstances.</p>

    <h2>Planning comes first</h2>
    <p>
      Every guide begins with the decision and the setting in which a product will be used.
      We encourage readers to understand the available space, access, installation requirements,
      compatibility, maintenance, future needs and practical constraints before considering a
      particular product.
    </p>
    <p>A well-planned purchase is more valuable than an impulse purchase.</p>

    <h2>AHC Product Research</h2>
    <p>
      AHC Product Research is a suitability-led review process. We do not assume there is one
      universal “best” product. We ask which households an option may suit, what compromises it
      brings and whether the available evidence is strong enough to support a recommendation.
    </p>

    <h3>Evidence we examine</h3>
    <p>A research-supported option draws on several distinct sources:</p>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li>manufacturer specifications, manuals and written warranty documents</li>
      <li>credible independent expert or technical reviews, where they add useful evidence</li>
      <li>recurring themes across a meaningful body of owner feedback</li>
      <li>Australian availability, warranty support, service access and fulfilment</li>
      <li>Australian regulator, warning and recall checks where relevant</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Manufacturer material can verify specifications but is not independent evidence of quality.
      A strong expert or technical review can add weight, but is not an automatic requirement or
      a substitute for practical Australian support and recurring owner experience. Retailer
      rankings, star averages and review counts do not establish suitability on their own. We
      qualify evidence that is limited, inconsistent or cannot be verified.
    </p>

    <h3>What readers should be able to see</h3>
    <p>Every promoted product assessment must make clear:</p>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li>who the product may suit and who it may not suit</li>
      <li>material drawbacks and recurring concerns</li>
      <li>the confidence we place in the available evidence</li>
      <li>when the product was last reviewed</li>
      <li>whether the assessment is research-based or includes genuine hands-on testing</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Unless we explicitly describe the testing conducted, Australian Home Collective has not
      personally tested the product. The assessment is based on research rather than hands-on use.
    </p>

    <h3>Hard exclusions</h3>
    <p>A product cannot receive promotional placement while any of these conditions applies:</p>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li>an active recall or unresolved safety concern</li>
      <li>a repeated pattern of serious reliability failures</li>
      <li>poor or unclear Australian warranty and support arrangements</li>
      <li>misleading or materially unverifiable claims</li>
      <li>suspicious, overly dependent or insufficient evidence</li>
      <li>an unsuitable seller, delivery or fulfilment route</li>
      <li>low-confidence evidence or a poor fit for the guide and intended reader</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Research outcomes</h3>
    <p>Each completed assessment receives one of four outcomes:</p>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li><strong>Research-supported option</strong> — the evidence and suitability case meet our publication standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Promising, but evidence is limited</strong> — the option may be useful, but the evidence is not yet strong enough for promotion.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixed evidence — not promoted</strong> — material concerns or conflicting evidence prevent promotion.</li>
      <li><strong>Does not meet our standard</strong> — the product fails a required threshold or triggers a hard exclusion.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Only a <strong>Research-supported option</strong> may receive promotional affiliate
      placement. It must also have clear safety and recall checks, moderate or high evidence
      confidence, a current research record and explicit approval for affiliate use. Approved
      records are reviewed at least every 180 days and sooner when material new evidence emerges.
    </p>

    <h2>How recommendations are expressed</h2>
    <p>
      Different homes, budgets and priorities often produce different reasonable choices. We
      explain which circumstances an option may suit, what trade-offs come with it and why the
      evidence supports the conclusion. Popularity, price, star ratings and commission rates do
      not determine inclusion.
    </p>

    <h2>Ratings and scores</h2>
    <p>
      Australian Home Collective does not assign arbitrary scores or Editor's Choice labels.
      If a structured score or comparison system is introduced in future, its criteria,
      weighting, evidence and limitations will be published clearly.
    </p>

    <h2>Commercial relationships</h2>
    <p>
      Affiliate or commercial relationships do not determine our methodology or conclusions.
      A commercial link can appear only after the product passes the research gate, receives
      explicit approval and carries the required disclosure. Products may be downgraded, paused or
      removed if new evidence changes the assessment. The surrounding guidance must remain useful
      when a reader does not use the link.
      Read our{" "}
      <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Editorial Independence & Affiliate Disclosure</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Interactive planning tools</h2>
    <p>
      Our tools help readers identify potential considerations and measurement conflicts. They
      do not provide engineering, installation, safety or delivery approval. Each tool should
      explain what it assesses, which information it needs, the assumptions it makes, what it
      cannot determine and when further verification is recommended.
    </p>

    <h2>Consumer rights information</h2>
    <p>
      Where ownership and warranty considerations are relevant, we may direct readers to
      authoritative information about Australian Consumer Law. We do not provide legal advice
      or decide how the law applies to an individual situation. See the ACCC's{" "}
      <ExternalLink href="https://www.accc.gov.au/consumers/buying-products-and-services/consumer-rights-and-guarantees">
        consumer rights and guarantees guidance
      </ExternalLink>.
    </p>

    <h2>Continuous improvement</h2>
    <p>
      We review and update content when guidance, standards, reader needs or our own practical
      understanding change. Substantive improvements may update the editorial review date.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Our guiding question is simple: will this help someone make a better decision for their home?</strong></p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
